From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Venkatakrishnaiah Pari <vpari@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 13:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] safelinux-modules: iommu_faults: use kzalloc instead of
 devm_kzalloc

match_and_register_fault() is invoked from iommu_fault_init() via
bus_for_each_dev() at module-load initcall time, for every platform
device whose device-tree node carries the boolean property
"iommu-faults" (e.g. sa8797's 9a00000.vfio_titan_base VFIO camera
passthrough node). This runs entirely independent of, and well before,
any driver ever probing or binding to that device.

create_device_fault_info() attaches its per-device
struct device_fault_info via devm_kzalloc(dev, ...). devm_* allocations
are only released by devres_release_all(), which is only reached from
device_unbind_cleanup() -- itself only called after a completed probe
attempt (success + later unbind/reprobe, or probe failure) or a real
driver unbind. None of those ever happen for a device this module
merely walks via bus_for_each_dev() and never binds a driver to.

The result is a devres entry that is permanently stuck on
dev->devres_head. really_probe() in drivers/base/dd.c refuses to probe
any device with a non-empty devres_head ("Resources present before
probing"), so every later attempt -- manual sysfs bind or automatic --
to bind e.g. vfio-platform to 9a00000.vfio_titan_base fails with -EBUSY:

  platform 9a00000.vfio_titan_base: Resources present before probing
  platform 9a00000.vfio_titan_base:   leftover devres: ... name=devm_kzalloc_release size=72 release=dr_node_release
  platform 9a00000.vfio_titan_base: bind_store: device_driver_attach failed with -16

Confirmed via a dump_stack() instrumented devm_kmalloc(): the
allocation happens at boot, from modprobe loading iommu_faults.ko,
through match_and_register_fault() -> devm_kmalloc(), before any bind
attempt is ever made.

Switch to a plain kzalloc()/kfree() on the error paths. This
allocation is intentionally kept alive for the life of the
fault-handler registration -- the module has no unload/teardown path
either (iommu_fault_exit() is a no-op) -- so it does not need to be
tied to a driver's devm lifecycle at all.

Change-Id: Ib998f00660ef227c62df4b580815171cf8d31e1a
Signed-off-by: Venkatakrishnaiah Pari <vpari@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 safelinux-modules/drivers/iommu_faults.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/safelinux-modules/drivers/iommu_faults.c b/safelinux-modules/drivers/iommu_faults.c
index 3795742..ff8ca21 100644
--- a/safelinux-modules/drivers/iommu_faults.c
+++ b/safelinux-modules/drivers/iommu_faults.c
@@ -172,7 +172,22 @@ static int create_device_fault_info(struct device *dev, void *data)
 	struct device_fault_info *dev_info;
 	struct iommu_domain *domain;
 
-	dev_info = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(struct device_fault_info), GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * This module registers a fault handler on arbitrary platform
+	 * devices via bus_for_each_dev() at module load time, well before
+	 * (and independent of) any driver ever probing/binding to @dev.
+	 * devm_* allocations are only released via devres_release_all(),
+	 * which is only called from device_unbind_cleanup() on a probe
+	 * failure/retry or a real driver unbind -- neither of which occurs
+	 * for a device that was never probed. Using devm_kzalloc() here
+	 * therefore leaves a permanent entry on dev->devres_head, which
+	 * makes really_probe() reject every future manual/automatic bind
+	 * attempt for @dev with -EBUSY ("Resources present before probing").
+	 * Use a plain kzalloc() instead; this allocation is intentionally
+	 * kept alive for the life of the fault-handler registration (the
+	 * module has no unload/teardown path either).
+	 */
+	dev_info = kzalloc(sizeof(struct device_fault_info), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!dev_info)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
@@ -185,6 +200,7 @@ static int create_device_fault_info(struct device *dev, void *data)
 	domain = iommu_get_dma_domain(dev);
 	if (!domain) {
 		dev_err(dev, "%s:iommu domain is NULL\n", __func__);
+		kfree(dev_info);
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
@@ -192,6 +208,8 @@ static int create_device_fault_info(struct device *dev, void *data)
 
 	if (sysfs_create_file(device_obj, &(dev_info->fault_attr.attr))) {
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to create sysfs file in /sys/kernel/\n");
+		iommu_set_fault_handler(domain, NULL, NULL);
+		kfree(dev_info);
 		return -ENOMEM;
 	}
 
-- 
2.34.1

